{"subjectId":"sql","id":"distinct-aliases","title":"DISTINCT & Aliases","group":"Querying Basics","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>DISTINCT</code> removes duplicate result rows; aliases give columns and tables short, readable names.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>DISTINCT</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT category FROM products ORDER BY category;\n\nSELECT DISTINCT city FROM customers ORDER BY city;       -- NULL counts as one value here</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>category\n-----------\nElectronics\nFurniture\nStationery\n\ncity\n---------\nNULL\nBengaluru\nDelhi\nMumbai</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>DISTINCT</code> applies to the <strong>whole row</strong>, not to one column:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, status FROM orders ORDER BY customer_id, status;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | status\n------------+----------\n1           | delivered\n1           | shipped\n2           | delivered\n3           | pending\n3           | shipped\n4           | cancelled</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers_who_ordered,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT status)      AS statuses\nFROM orders;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customers_who_ordered | statuses\n----------------------+---------\n4                     | 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Reaching for <code>DISTINCT</code> to hide duplicates caused by a join usually hides a bug (a missing join condition or a one-to-many join). Fix the join — or use <code>EXISTS</code> — instead.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL (DISTINCT ON)</figcaption><pre><code>-- PostgreSQL only: first row per group (by the ORDER BY)\nSELECT DISTINCT ON (dept_id) dept_id, name, salary\nFROM employees\nORDER BY dept_id, salary DESC;</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Column aliases</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name AS employee,\n       salary * 12 AS \"annual pay\",       -- double quotes: spaces or reserved words\n       hire_date joined                    -- AS is optional\nFROM employees\nWHERE emp_id &lt;= 2\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>employee | annual pay | joined\n---------+------------+-----------\nAsha     | 1800000    | 2015-03-01\nRavi     | 1440000    | 2017-06-15</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table-alias\"></i>Table aliases</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT e.name, d.name AS department\nFROM employees AS e\nJOIN departments d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nWHERE e.salary &gt; 100000\nORDER BY e.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | department\n------+------------\nAsha  | Engineering\nRavi  | Engineering\nPriya | Sales</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A table alias is required when the same table appears twice (self-join) and makes every multi-table query readable. Oracle does not accept <code>AS</code> before a <em>table</em> alias.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A column alias cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code> of the same query (the <code>SELECT</code> list is evaluated <em>after</em> <code>WHERE</code>) — see the next topic. SQLite and MySQL relax this in places, which is not portable.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between DISTINCT and GROUP BY?</summary><div><p><code>SELECT DISTINCT a, b</code> and <code>SELECT a, b … GROUP BY a, b</code> return the same rows and usually get the same plan. Use <code>DISTINCT</code> to de-duplicate; use <code>GROUP BY</code> when you also compute aggregates per group.</p></div></details></section>"}